-- erwartet: C001
-- **666 -- a `reason` value no C enumerator can carry.**
--
-- A `reason` lowers to `typedef enum { … } R;` -- *"eine benannte Zahlenmenge, und die
-- Zahlen STEHEN DA"*, `emit.rs` «C3a». C11 6.7.2.2p2 is a CONSTRAINT on what may stand
-- there: an enumeration constant *shall be an integer constant expression that has a value
-- representable as an `int`*. The emitter wrote whatever the source named.
--
-- Three complaints over one cause, all found by `instrumente/fuzze-erzeuger.py` on
-- 2026-09-02 and all still standing on 2026-09-03:
--
--     A = 2147483648            cc -Wpedantic: ISO C restricts enumerator values to range
--                               of `int`                            13 cases, net 5
--     A = 18446744073709551615  cc -Werror: integer constant is so large that it is
--                               unsigned                             6 cases, shape 2
--     A = 2^127                 cc -Werror: integer constant is too large for its type
--                                                                   10 cases, shape 2
--
-- **The value below is the first one C cannot carry** -- `INT_MAX + 1`, and nothing more.
-- It is deliberately not the largest: the largest one draws `-Werror` from the plain gate,
-- and a probe that falls at the LOUDEST reader does not measure the quiet one. This value
-- passes `cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror` in silence and is refused only by `-Wpedantic`
-- -- which is the gate `tests/beispiele.rs` does not run.
--
-- THE DECISION, 2026-09-03 -- refused, and NOT suffixed
-- ----------------------------------------------------
-- `A = 18446744073709551615u` silences `-Werror` and leaves the constraint violation exactly
-- where it stood; the failure merely moves to `-Wpedantic`. **That is `D6`'s argument one
-- file over: a repair that moves the complaint to a quieter tool is not a repair.** So the
-- fence stands where C's own rule stands, and the number in the refusal is GCC's.
--
-- > *`D3` named nine slots and repaired seven.* `reason` was one of the two it missed, and
-- > the missing suffix was never the whole of what was wrong here: an enumerator is the one
-- > C object whose width the emitter does not choose. `messung/ERZEUGERREST.md` §3.

module gift::reason_value_past_the_c_enumerator {

reason Ausfall {
    ZuGross = 2147483648 "the first value C cannot put in an enumerator"
    exhaustive
}

}
